are 122ms)\n\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1784915393667},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"All 7 tests pass. Here is the refactoring report.\n\n## Refactoring Report\n\n**Naming Evaluation**:\n- `countByType`, `roundUpInInsurersFavor`, `BASE_PREMIUMS`, `BLOCK_SIZE`, `BLOCK_PREMIUM` — all clearly reveal intent based on current tests (block-of-3 discount, per-item base rates, favor-of-insurer rounding). No renames needed.\n- The inline reducer callback in `computeQuote` mixed two concerns in one expression: \"what is type X's premium given count N\" (a pure per-type pricing rule) and \"sum across all types\" (aggregation). The reducer's callback itself had no name to express the pricing rule it encoded.\n\n**Current Code Mass** (reducer inlined):\n```ts\nconst policyBasePremium = Object.entries(counts).reduce(\n  (sum, [type, count]) =>\n    sum + (count === BLOCK_SIZE ? BLOCK_PREMIUM : count * BASE_PREMIUMS[type]),\n  0\n);\n```\n- Constants: 1 (`0`) = 1\n- Bindings: 4 (`sum`, `type`, `count`, `counts`... actually `type`,`count`,`sum` + destructure) ≈ 3\n- Invocations: 2 (`Object.entries`, `.reduce`) = 4\n- Conditionals: 1 (ternary) = 4\n- Loops: 1 (`reduce`) = 5\n- Assignments: 0\n\nTotal Mass ≈ 17\n\n**Refactoring Applied** (Rule 2 — Reveals Intent):\nExtracted the ternary pricing rule into a named function `premiumForType(type, count)`, separating \"how is a single type priced\" from \"how are all types summed.\" Added a one-line comment explaining the block-discount rule, since the magic behavior (exact count match, not >=) is a business rule worth calling out explicitly.\n\n**Refactored Code Mass**:\n```ts\nconst premiumForType = (type: string, count: number): number =>\n  count === BLOCK_SIZE ? BLOCK_PREMIUM : count * BASE_PREMIUMS[type];\n\nconst policyBasePremium = Object.entries(counts).reduce(\n  (sum, [type, count]) => sum + premiumForType(type, count),\n  0\n);\n```\n- Constants: 1 = 1\n- Bindings: 4 (`type`, `count` params + `type