le`: still accurately describes \"amount - DEDUCTIBLE\" for a single damage — no change needed.\n- `computeClaim`: still accurately describes the overall operation — no change needed.\n- The inline `damages.reduce(...)` inside `computeClaim`, however, had no name of its own — the *concept* \"sum every damage's deducted payout\" was only implicit in an anonymous reduce callback, unlike quote.ts's convention of naming every aggregation step (`sumBasePremiums`, `sumItemSurcharges`).\n- Decision: **extract and name** the reduce as `sumDeductedPayouts`, matching the established naming convention in the sibling module (`sum*` for aggregation helpers) and making `computeClaim`'s body read as a single declarative sentence.\n\n**Current Code Mass (before)**:\n```ts\nexport const computeClaim = (\n  policyItems: QuoteItem[],\n  damages: Damage[],\n  remainingCap: number\n): ClaimResult => {\n  const payout = damages.reduce(\n    (sum, damage) => sum + applyDeductible(damage.amount),\n    0\n  );\n  return { payout, remainingCap: remainingCap - payout };\n};\n```\n- Constants: 1 (`0`) = 1\n- Bindings: 6 (`policyItems`, `damages`, `remainingCap`, `payout`, `sum`, `damage`) = 6\n- Invocations: 2 (`reduce`, `applyDeductible`) = 4\n- Conditionals: 0 = 0\n- Loops: 1 (`reduce`) = 5\n- Assignments: 0 (all `const`) = 0\n\nTotal Mass: **16**\n\n**Refactored Code Mass (after)**:\n```ts\nconst sumDeductedPayouts = (damages: Damage[]): number =>\n  damages.reduce((sum, damage) => sum + applyDeductible(damage.amount), 0);\n\nexport const computeClaim = (\n  policyItems: QuoteItem[],\n  damages: Damage[],\n  remainingCap: number\n): ClaimResult => {\n  const payout = sumDeductedPayouts(damages);\n  return { payout, remainingCap: remainingCap - payout };\n};\n```\n- Constants: 1 (`0`) = 1\n- Bindings: 7 (`damages` x2, `sum`, `damage`, `policyItems`, `remainingCap`, `payout`) = 7\n- Invocations: 3 (`reduce`, `applyDeductible`, `sumDeductedPayouts`) = 6\n- Conditionals: 0 = 0\n- Loops: 1 (`red