\n};\n\nconst sumDeductedPayouts = (\n  policyItems: QuoteItem[],\n  damages: Damage[]\n): number =>\n  damages.reduce((sum, damage) => sum + payoutFor(policyItems, damage), 0);\n\nconst validateDamagesFitPolicy = (\n  policyItems: QuoteItem[],\n  damages: Damage[]\n): void => {\n  const insuredCounts = countByType(policyItems);\n  const damageCounts = countByType(\n    damages.map((damage) => ({ type: damage.itemType }))\n  );\n  for (const [itemType, count] of Object.entries(damageCounts)) {\n    if (count > (insuredCounts[itemType] ?? 0)) {\n      throw new Error(\n        `More damage entries for '${itemType}' than the policy covers`\n      );\n    }\n  }\n};\n\nexport const computeClaim = (\n  policyItems: QuoteItem[],\n  damages: Damage[],\n  remainingCap: number\n): ClaimResult => {\n  validateDamagesFitPolicy(policyItems, damages);\n  const payout = sumDeductedPayouts(policyItems, damages);\n  return { payout, remainingCap: remainingCap - payout };\n};\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1784917891822},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Refactoring Complete report:\n\n## Refactoring Report\n\n**Naming Evaluation**:\n- Current name: `validateDamagesFitPolicy(policyItems, damages)`\n- Function purpose: counts damages and insured items by type, throws if any damage type's count exceeds its insured count.\n- Assessment: name clearly reveals intent (\"fits policy\" captures the count-comparison check). No rename needed.\n- `countByType`: name is accurate and generic in both files — good candidate for dedup (see Rule 3 below).\n\n**Rule 1 (Tests Pass)**: All 30 tests were green before refactoring. Confirmed via `pnpm test`.\n\n**Rule 3 (No Duplication) — the improvement applied**:\n`claim.ts` had its own local `countByType` helper that was structurally identical to the one already in `quote.ts` (only the parameter type annotation differed: `{ type: string }[]` vs `QuoteItem[]`, but `QuoteItem` already has a mandatory `type: string` field, so `Quote