Id":"rqsty-cmpl-dbb5384f-2c4f-4b62-8a2e-6ebc3cf6d3f6","responseModel":"claude-sonnet-5"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"toolu_vrtx_01TZRYAqdnLsvH2mYwUg3T3A","toolName":"bash","content":[{"type":"text","text":"\n> tdd-experiment-run@ test /home/experimenter/experiments/runs/2026-07-24_17-26-10_claim-office-example-mapping_v6.2-with-why-cleaned-pi_sonnet-5-no-thinking\n> vitest run\n\n\n RUN  v1.6.1 /home/experimenter/experiments/runs/2026-07-24_17-26-10_claim-office-example-mapping_v6.2-with-why-cleaned-pi_sonnet-5-no-thinking\n\n ✓ src/quote.spec.ts  (21 tests) 4ms\n ✓ src/claim.spec.ts  (15 tests) 3ms\n ↓ src/scenario.spec.ts  (8 tests | 8 skipped)\n\n Test Files  2 passed | 1 skipped (3)\n      Tests  36 passed | 8 todo (44)\n   Start at  18:40:23\n   Duration  415ms (transform 48ms, setup 0ms, collect 56ms, tests 7ms, environment 0ms, prepare 124ms)\n\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1784918424357},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"All 36 tests still green. Let me view the final file for the report."},{"type":"toolCall","id":"toolu_vrtx_01C8HB5Srf8oLJxon6jruexC","name":"read","arguments":{"path":"src/claim.ts"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"requesty","model":"vertex/claude-sonnet-5@eu","usage":{"input":8248,"output":72,"cacheRead":10064,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":0,"totalTokens":18384,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1784918424358,"responseId":"rqsty-cmpl-c5015095-bccc-4232-a4fd-fa7a7aa64f37","responseModel":"claude-sonnet-5"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"toolu_vrtx_01C8HB5Srf8oLJxon6jruexC","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"import { QuoteItem, countByType } from \"./quote.js\";\nimport { avoidFloatNoise } from \"./rounding.js\";\n\nexport interface Damage {\n  itemType: string;\n  amount: number;\n}\n\nexport interface ClaimResult {\n  payout: number;\n  remainingCap: number;\n}\n\nconst DEDUCTIBLE = 100;\n\n// Names the single arithmetic step the claim payout depends on today,\n// mirroring the small-named-helper style used throughout quote.ts.\nconst applyDeductible = (amount: number): number => amount - DEDUCTIBLE;\n\nconst HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_THRESHOLD = 8;\nconst HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_REIMBURSEMENT_RATE = 0.5;\nconst FULL_REIMBURSEMENT_RATE = 1;\n\nconst findPolicyItem = (\n  policyItems: QuoteItem[],\n  itemType: string\n): QuoteItem | undefined =>\n  policyItems.find((item) => item.type === itemType);\n\nconst reimbursementRateFor = (item: QuoteItem | undefined): number =>\n  (item?.enchantment ?? 0) >= HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_THRESHOLD\n    ? HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_REIMBURSEMENT_RATE\n    : FULL_REIMBURSEMENT_RATE;\n\nconst payoutFor = (policyItems: QuoteItem[], damage: Damage): number => {\n  const item = findPolicyItem(policyItems, damage.itemType);\n  const rate = reimbursementRateFor(item);\n  const reimbursedAmount = damage.amount * rate;\n  return applyDeductible(reimbursedAmount);\n};\n\nconst sumDeductedPayouts = (\n  policyItems: QuoteItem[],\n  damages: Damage[]\n): number =>\n  damages.reduce((sum, damage) => sum + payoutFor(policyItems, damage), 0);\n\n// A damage amount is meaningless below zero regardless of what the\n// policy covers -- this is a data-integrity check, not a policy rule.\nconst validateNoNegativeDamageAmounts = (damages: Damage[]): void => {\n  for (const damage of damages) {\n    if (damage.amount < 0) {\n      throw new Error(`Damage amount cannot be negative: ${damage.amount}`);\n    }\n  }\n};\n\n// The policy rule: a claim can't reference more damaged items of a\n// given type than the policy actually insures.\nconst validateDamageCountsFitPolicy = (\n  policyItems: QuoteItem[],\n  damages: Damage[]\n): void => {\n  const insuredCounts = countByType(policyItems);\n  const damageCounts = countByType(\n    damages.map((damage) => ({ type: damage.itemType }))\n  );\n  for (const [itemType, count] of Object.entries(damageCounts)) {\n    if (count > (insuredCounts[itemType] ?? 0)) {\n      throw new Error(\n        `More damage entries for '${itemType}' than the policy covers`\n      );\n    }\n  }\n};\n\nconst validateDamages = (\n  policyItems: QuoteItem[],\n  damages: Damage[]\n): void => {\n  validateNoNegativeDamageAmounts(damages);\n  validateDamageCountsFitPolicy(policyItems, damages);\n};\n\n// Names the domain rule that a claim's payout can never exceed what's\n// left of the policy's insurance cap, mirroring the small-named-helper\n// style used for applyDeductible above.\nconst capPayoutAtRemainingLimit = (\n  desiredPayout: number,\n  remainingCap: number\n): number => Math.min(desiredPayout, remainingCap);\n\nconst roundDownInInsurersFavor = (amount: number): number =>\n  Math.floor(avoidFloatNoise(amount));\n\nexport const computeClaim = (\n  policyItems: QuoteItem[],\n  damages: Damage[],\n  remainingCap: number\n): ClaimResult => {\n  validateDamages(policyItems, damages);\n  const desiredPayout = sumDeductedPayouts(policyItems, damages);\n  const payout = roundDownInInsurersFavor(\n    capPayoutAtRemainingLimit(desiredPayout, remainingCap)\n  );\n  return { payout, remainingCap: remainingCap - payout };\n};\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1784918426050},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"## Refactoring Report\n\n**Naming Evaluation (First Priority)**:\n- Current name under review: `validateDamagesFitPolicy`\n- Function's actual behavior after the last Green phase: it now does *two conceptually distinct* things — (1) reject any negative damage amount, (2) reject damage counts per type that exceed what the policy insures.\n- Question: Does \"validateDamagesFitPolicy\" clearly reveal both jobs? \"Fit policy\" strongly evokes the *count-vs-policy* check, but the negative-amount check has nothing to do with the policy at all — it's a basic data-integrity rule that would apply even to a policy that insures nothing.\n- Assessment: The name no longer describes the full intent of the function; it silently gained a second, unrelated responsibility.\n- Decision: **Split** `validateDamagesFitPolicy` into two single-purpose functions (`validateNoNegativeDamageAmounts`, `validateDamageCountsFitPolicy`) composed by a small orchestrator `validateDamages`. Each new name precisely matches what its function checks.\n\n**Rule 1 — Tests Pass**: All 36 tests (15 in `claim.spec.ts`, 21 in `quote.spec.ts`) were green before and after. Verified via `pnpm test` after the change.\n\n**Rule 2 — Reveals Intent**: This was the deciding factor here (see naming evaluation above). The two validation rules are different in kind — one is a basic invariant on the `Damage` shape, the other is a cross-checking policy rule — and now each has its own name and comment explaining *why* it exists, not just what it does.\n\n**Rule 3 — No Duplication**: No duplication found elsewhere in the file; `findPolicyItem`, `reimbursementRateFor`, `payoutFor`, `capPayoutAtRemainingLimit`, and the rounding helpers each represent a single piece of domain knowledge with no repeated logic.\n\n**Rule 4 — Fewest Elements**: The split adds one extra top-level binding (`validateDamages` orchestrator) compared to the single combined function, but this is justified by Rule 2 — APP mass is not the deciding factor when clarity is at stake.\n\n**APP Mass**:\n\nBefore (`validateDamagesFitPolicy`, single function):\n- Constants: `0` (literal) = 1, string template = 1 → 2\n- Bindings: `policyItems`, `damages`, `damage`, `insuredCounts`, `damageCounts`, `itemType`, `count` = 7\n- Invocations: `countByType` x2, `.map`, `Object.entries` = 4 → 8\n- Conditionals: `damage.amount < 0`, `count > (...)` = 2 → 8\n- Loops: `for...of` x2 = 2 → 10\n- Assignments: 0\n- **Total: 2 + 7 + 8 + 8 + 10 = 35**\n\nAfter (three functions: `validateNoNegativeDamageAmounts` + `validateDamageCountsFitPolicy` + `validateDamages`):\n- Constants: 2 (same literals, now split across two functions)\n- Bindings: `damages`(x2 params across two fns) + `damage` + `policyItems` + `insuredCounts` + `damageCounts` + `itemType` + `count` + orchestrator params `policyItems`, `damages` = 9\n- Invocations: `countByType` x2, `.map`, `Object.entries`, plus 2 new invocations of the extracted functions from the orchestrator = 6 → 12\n- Conditionals: 2 → 8\n- Loops: 2 → 10\n- Assignments: 0\n- **Total: 2 + 9 + 12 + 8 + 10 = 41**\n\nMass Change: 35 → 41 (+6). Mass increased slightly because of the extra orchestration layer and its two invocation calls, but per the guidelines, **Rule 2 (clarity) trumps APP** — the previous single function was a misnamed, dual-purpose block that would only get harder to name correctly as more validation rules are added (e.g., a future rule about policy-vs-damage that isn't a count check). Splitting now keeps each function's name honest and sets up a clean seam for future rules to be added as new sibling checks rather than more branches jammed into one function.\n\n**Refactoring Applied**:\n- Renamed the misleading `validateDamagesFitPolicy` by splitting it into `validateNoNegativeDamageAmounts` and `validateDamageCountsFitPolicy`, unified under a small `validateDamages` orchestrator called from `computeClaim`.\n- Added a one-line \"why\" comment to each new function distinguishing \"data-integrity check\" from \"policy rule.\"\n\nRefactoring Complete:\n**Refactoring**: Split `validateDamagesFitPolicy` into two single-responsibility, correctly-named validators (`validateNoNegativeDamageAmounts`, `validateDamageCountsFitPolicy`) plus an orchestrator (`validateDamages`), driven by Rule 2 (naming/intent) taking priority over Rule 4/APP.\n**Mass Change**: 35 -> 41 (+6, accepted for clarity per Rule 2 > APP)\n**Tests**: All 36 passing (15 claim.spec.ts, 21 quote.spec.ts, 8 scenario.spec.ts todo/skipped)"}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"requesty","model":"vertex/claude-sonnet-5@eu","usage":{"input":9801,"output":1807,"cacheRead":10064,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":0,"totalTokens":21672,"cost":{"input":0,"outpu