hat policies have a cap it can ask for.\n- Exported `capFor` from `claim.ts`; `scenario.ts` now only imports and calls it.\n- Rule 3 (DRY): no duplication found or introduced.\n- Rule 4 (Fewest elements): no unnecessary abstractions found; nothing removed or added beyond relocating.\n\nBenefits:\n- `scenario.ts` is now solely step-orchestration (quote/claim dispatch + `PolicyState`), matching its own describe-block name.\n- `claim.ts` is now the single source of truth for all claim/cap domain rules, colocated with `capPayoutAtRemainingLimit` and `DEDUCTIBLE`.\n- Future changes to insurance values or cap logic only touch one file, and that file is the one whose tests actually exercise those rules (`claim.spec.ts` conceptually, though currently covered via `scenario.spec.ts`).\n\nRefactoring Complete:\n**Refactoring**: Moved `INSURANCE_VALUES`/`insuranceSumOf`/`capFor` from `scenario.ts` into `claim.ts` (Rule 2 — module-level intent clarity); confirmed existing function names already fit their purpose.\n**Mass Change**: 21 -> 21 (delta 0; this was a Rule 2 clarity win, not a mass reduction)\n**Tests**: All 40 passing, 4 todo unaffected, `tsc --noEmit` clean."}],"details":{"mode":"single","agentScope":"both","projectAgentsDir":"/home/experimenter/experiments/runs/2026-07-24_17-26-10_claim-office-example-mapping_v6.2-with-why-cleaned-pi_sonnet-5-no-thinking/.pi/agents","results":[{"agent":"refactor","agentSource":"project","task":"Test file: src/scenario.spec.ts\nImplementation file: src/scenario.ts\nPassing tests: 4 in scenario.spec.ts (40 total)\nRecent changes: Three consecutive tests (sword+amulet cap, cursed sword cap based on unmodified insurance value, sword+3-runes-block cap unaffected by premium block discount) all passed without needing implementation changes -- the existing insuranceSumOf/capFor helpers already correctly summed raw INSURANCE_VALUES regardless of premium modifiers or block discounts.\n\nRefactor the implementation while keeping all tests green